      <!-- ===== CARDS ===== -->
      <div id="greenhouse-effect" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">1</span><h2>Greenhouse Effect — ग्रीनहाउस प्रभाव</h2></div><p><strong>Greenhouse Effect</strong> वह natural process है जिसमें <strong>greenhouse gases</strong> Earth's atmosphere में <strong>heat</strong> को trap करते हैं — Earth को warm and habitable बनाते हैं।</p><div class="formula-box">Greenhouse Effect = Sun's Energy + Earth's Radiation + GHG Trap Heat</div><p><strong>How it Works:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Incoming solar radiation:</strong> Sun's energy reaches Earth</li><li><strong>Earth absorbs and re-emits:</strong> Earth's surface warms and emits infrared radiation (heat)</li><li><strong>GHG trap heat:</strong> Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit infrared radiation, trapping heat in the atmosphere</li><li><strong>Natural vs Enhanced:</strong> Natural greenhouse effect is essential for life; enhanced effect (due to human activities) causes global warming</li></ul></div>

      <div id="greenhouse-gases" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">2</span><h2>Greenhouse Gases (GHGs) — ग्रीनहाउस गैसें</h2></div><p><strong>Greenhouse Gases</strong> वे gases हैं जो <strong>infrared radiation</strong> को absorb और re-emit करती हैं — causing the greenhouse effect.</p><p><strong>Major GHGs:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>CO₂ (Carbon Dioxide) — कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड:</strong> Most abundant GHG; from fossil fuel burning, deforestation</li><li><strong>CH₄ (Methane) — मीथेन:</strong> 25x more potent than CO₂; from agriculture, livestock, landfills</li><li><strong>N₂O (Nitrous Oxide) — नाइट्रस ऑक्साइड:</strong> 298x more potent than CO₂; from agriculture (fertilisers), industrial processes</li><li><strong>CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons) — CFC:</strong> Ozone-depleting and potent GHGs; from refrigerants, aerosols</li><li><strong>Water Vapour (H₂O) — जल वाष्प:</strong> Most abundant GHG; natural</li><li><strong>Ozone (O₃) — ओजोन:</strong> Tropospheric ozone is a GHG</li></ul><p><strong>GWP (Global Warming Potential):</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>CO₂ = 1 (baseline)</li><li>CH₄ = 25</li><li>N₂O = 298</li><li>CFCs = Thousands</li></ul></div>

      <div id="global-warming" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">3</span><h2>Global Warming — वैश्विक तापन</h2></div><p><strong>Global Warming</strong> Earth's average temperature में <strong>long-term increase</strong> है — primarily due to <strong>enhanced greenhouse effect</strong> caused by human activities.</p><div class="formula-box">Global Warming = Enhanced GHG + Trapped Heat + Rising Temperature</div><p><strong>Causes:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Burning of fossil fuels (CO₂ emissions)</li><li>Deforestation (reduces CO₂ absorption)</li><li>Agriculture and livestock (CH₄, N₂O)</li><li>Industrial processes (GHG emissions)</li><li>Waste decomposition (CH₄)</li></ul><p><strong>Effects:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Rising global temperatures</li><li>Melting ice caps and glaciers</li><li>Sea level rise</li><li>Extreme weather events</li><li>Ocean acidification</li></ul></div>

      <div id="climate-change" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">4</span><h2>Climate Change — जलवायु परिवर्तन</h2></div><p><strong>Climate Change</strong> weather patterns में <strong>long-term shift</strong> है — temperature, precipitation, wind patterns में changes — primarily due to global warming.</p><div class="formula-box">Climate Change = Global Warming + Weather Pattern Shifts + Long-term Changes</div><p><strong>Difference: Global Warming vs Climate Change</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Global Warming:</strong> Increase in average global temperature</li><li><strong>Climate Change:</strong> Broader concept — includes temperature changes + precipitation, wind, storms, etc.</li></ul><p><strong>Key Observations:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Rising global average temperature</li><li>Changing precipitation patterns</li><li>Increasing frequency of extreme weather</li><li>Melting ice and rising sea levels</li></ul></div>

      <div id="climate-impacts" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">5</span><h2>Climate Change Impacts — जलवायु परिवर्तन के प्रभाव</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Environmental Impacts:</strong><ul><li>Melting glaciers and ice sheets</li><li>Rising sea levels</li><li>Ocean acidification</li><li>Loss of biodiversity</li><li>Changes in ecosystems and habitats</li></ul></li><li><strong>Social/Economic Impacts:</strong><ul><li>Agriculture and food security</li><li>Water scarcity</li><li>Health impacts (heat stress, disease spread)</li><li>Climate refugees</li><li>Economic losses (damage to infrastructure, reduced productivity)</li></ul></li><li><strong>Extreme Weather Events:</strong><ul><li>More frequent and intense heatwaves</li><li>Heavy rainfall and floods</li><li>Droughts and wildfires</li><li>Stronger cyclones and storms</li></ul></li></ul></div>

      <div id="mitigation-adaptation" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">6</span><h2>Mitigation & Adaptation — शमन एवं अनुकूलन</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Mitigation — शमन:</strong> Greenhouse gas emissions को <strong>reduce</strong> या <strong>prevent</strong> करना — addressing the <strong>cause</strong> of climate change.<ul><li>Renewable energy (solar, wind)</li><li>Energy efficiency</li><li>Reforestation and afforestation</li><li>Carbon capture and storage</li><li>Reducing deforestation</li><li>Transition to low-carbon economy</li></ul></li><li><strong>Adaptation — अनुकूलन:</strong> Climate change के <strong>effects</strong> को manage करना — adjusting to the <strong>consequences</strong>.<ul><li>Building flood defences</li><li>Drought-resistant crops</li><li>Early warning systems</li><li>Climate-resilient infrastructure</li><li>Water conservation</li></ul></li></ul></div>

      <div id="climate-justice" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">7</span><h2>Climate Justice — जलवायु न्याय</h2></div><p><strong>Climate Justice</strong> का अर्थ है कि climate change के <strong>impacts</strong> और <strong>responsibilities</strong> को <strong>fairly</strong> distributed किया जाए — especially for vulnerable and marginalised communities.</p><div class="formula-box">Climate Justice = Fair Distribution + Vulnerable Protection + Equitable Responsibility</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Climate change affects <strong>poor and vulnerable</strong> communities the most</li><li>Developed countries have contributed <strong>most emissions</strong></li><li>Developing countries have <strong>limited resources</strong> to adapt</li><li>Climate justice demands <strong>equity</strong> in mitigation and adaptation</li><li>Includes <strong>intergenerational</strong> and <strong>intragenerational</strong> equity</li></ul></div>

      <div id="cbdr-rc" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">8</span><h2>CBDR-RC — समान परंतु विभेदित उत्तरदायित्व</h2></div><p><strong>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities (CBDR-RC)</strong> — climate action में <strong>all countries</strong> have common responsibility, but <strong>differentiated</strong> based on their <strong>historical emissions</strong> and <strong>capabilities</strong>.</p><div class="formula-box">CBDR-RC = Common Responsibility + Differentiated Action + Respective Capabilities</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>All countries must act on climate change</li><li>Developed countries have <strong>greater responsibility</strong> (historically emitted more)</li><li>Developing countries have <strong>less capacity</strong> and need <strong>support</strong></li><li>Developed countries should provide <strong>climate finance</strong> and <strong>technology transfer</strong></li><li>Included in <strong>UNFCCC</strong> and <strong>Paris Agreement</strong></li></ul></div>

      <div id="ozone-depletion" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">9</span><h2>Ozone Depletion — ओजोन क्षरण</h2></div><p><strong>Ozone Depletion</strong> stratospheric <strong>ozone layer</strong> का <strong>thinning</strong> है — primarily caused by <strong>CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons)</strong> and other ozone-depleting substances (ODS).</p><div class="formula-box">Ozone Depletion = CFCs + UV Radiation + Ozone Layer Thinning</div><p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Ozone layer:</strong> Protects Earth from harmful UV-B radiation</li><li><strong>CFCs:</strong> Used in refrigerants, aerosols, solvents</li><li><strong>Other ODS:</strong> Halons, methyl bromide, carbon tetrachloride</li><li><strong>Ozone hole:</strong> Thinning over Antarctica (discovered 1985)</li></ul><p><strong>Effects:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Increased UV radiation reaches Earth</li><li>Skin cancer and cataracts in humans</li><li>Damage to plants and marine ecosystems</li></ul><p><strong>Montreal Protocol (1987):</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>International treaty to phase out ODS</li><li>Most successful environmental treaty</li><li>Ozone layer is recovering</li></ul></div>

      <div id="ocean-acidification" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">10</span><h2>Ocean Acidification — महासागरीय अम्लीकरण</h2></div><p><strong>Ocean Acidification</strong> ocean water का <strong>pH decrease</strong> है — due to absorption of atmospheric CO₂.</p><div class="formula-box">Ocean Acidification = CO₂ + Ocean + pH Decrease</div><p><strong>How it Works:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Atmospheric CO₂ dissolves in ocean water</li><li>Forms carbonic acid (H₂CO₃)</li><li>Releases H⁺ ions → pH decreases (more acidic)</li><li>Impacts marine organisms</li></ul><p><strong>Effects:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Shell-forming organisms (corals, shellfish, plankton) — their calcium carbonate shells dissolve</li><li>Coral bleaching and reef degradation</li><li>Disruption of marine food webs</li><li>Reduced fish populations</li></ul></div>

      <div id="biodiversity-loss" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">11</span><h2>Biodiversity Loss — जैव विविधता हानि</h2></div><p><strong>Biodiversity</strong> Earth पर life की <strong>variety</strong> है — genes, species, ecosystems. <strong>Biodiversity Loss</strong> इस variety का <strong>decline</strong> है.</p><div class="formula-box">Biodiversity = Species + Genes + Ecosystems | Loss = Decline in Variety</div><p><strong>Causes:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Habitat destruction — आवास विनाश:</strong> Deforestation, urbanisation, agriculture</li><li><strong>Climate Change — जलवायु परिवर्तन:</strong> Alters habitats and ranges</li><li><strong>Pollution — प्रदूषण:</strong> Air, water, soil pollution</li><li><strong>Overexploitation — अत्यधिक दोहन:</strong> Overfishing, hunting, poaching</li><li><strong>Invasive Species — आक्रामक प्रजातियाँ:</strong> Non-native species disrupting ecosystems</li></ul><p><strong>Impacts:</strong></p><ul class="bullet-list"><li>Loss of ecosystem services</li><li>Food and water insecurity</li><li>Medicinal resource loss</li><li>Ecosystem instability</li></ul></div>

      <div id="international-agreements" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">12</span><h2>International Agreements — अंतर्राष्ट्रीय समझौते</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>UNFCCC (1992) — संयुक्त राष्ट्र जलवायु परिवर्तन फ्रेमवर्क कन्वेंशन:</strong> Framework for climate change action; Rio Summit 1992; CBDR principle</li><li><strong>Kyoto Protocol (1997) — क्योटो प्रोटोकॉल:</strong> Legally binding emission reduction targets for developed countries (Annex I countries)</li><li><strong>Paris Agreement (2015) — पेरिस समझौता:</strong> Global climate agreement — all countries; goal to limit warming to 1.5°C-2°C; NDCs (Nationally Determined Contributions)</li><li><strong>Montreal Protocol (1987) — मॉन्ट्रियल प्रोटोकॉल:</strong> Phase out ozone-depleting substances (CFCs)</li><li><strong>CBD (1992) — Convention on Biological Diversity:</strong> Conservation of biodiversity, sustainable use, equitable sharing of benefits</li></ul></div>

      <div id="exam-traps" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">13</span><h2>Common Exam Traps — सामान्य परीक्षा जाल</h2></div><ul class="bullet-list"><li><strong>Trap 1:</strong> Global warming = climate change → ❌ Global warming is one aspect of climate change.</li><li><strong>Trap 2:</strong> Ozone depletion = global warming → ❌ Ozone depletion is caused by CFCs; global warming is caused by GHGs.</li><li><strong>Trap 3:</strong> CO₂ is the most potent GHG → ❌ CO₂ is most abundant but less potent than CH₄, N₂O, CFCs.</li><li><strong>Trap 4:</strong> Ocean acidification = ocean pollution → ❌ Ocean acidification is caused by CO₂ absorption, not pollution.</li><li><strong>Trap 5:</strong> CBDR = all countries equal responsibility → ❌ CBDR means differentiated responsibility based on historical emissions.</li><li><strong>Trap 6:</strong> Montreal Protocol = climate change → ❌ Montreal Protocol is for ozone depletion (CFCs).</li><li><strong>Trap 7:</strong> Paris Agreement = legally binding targets → ❌ Paris Agreement uses NDCs (voluntary, not legally binding targets).</li></ul></div>

      <div id="practice-questions" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">14</span><h2>Practice Questions — अभ्यास प्रश्न</h2></div>
      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 1</div>
      <p>Which of the following is NOT a greenhouse gas?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. CO₂</li><li>B. CH₄</li><li>C. O₂</li><li>D. N₂O</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: C. O₂</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: O₂ (oxygen) is not a greenhouse gas. CO₂, CH₄, and N₂O are GHGs.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 2</div>
      <p>The Montreal Protocol is associated with:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Climate change</li><li>B. Ozone depletion</li><li>C. Biodiversity</li><li>D. Desertification</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Ozone depletion</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The Montreal Protocol (1987) was designed to phase out ozone-depleting substances (CFCs).</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 3</div>
      <p>CBDR-RC is a principle associated with:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Ozone depletion</li><li>B. Climate change</li><li>C. Biodiversity</li><li>D. Desertification</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. Climate change</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: CBDR-RC (Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities) is a key principle of climate change governance (UNFCCC, Paris Agreement).</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 4</div>
      <p>Which gas has the highest Global Warming Potential (GWP)?</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. CO₂</li><li>B. CH₄</li><li>C. N₂O</li><li>D. CFCs</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: D. CFCs</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: CFCs have the highest GWP (thousands of times more potent than CO₂). N₂O is 298x, CH₄ is 25x, CO₂ is 1x.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 5</div>
      <p>Ocean acidification is caused by:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. Excess nutrients</li><li>B. CO₂ absorption</li><li>C. Oil spills</li><li>D. Plastic waste</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: B. CO₂ absorption</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: Ocean acidification is caused by the absorption of atmospheric CO₂, which forms carbonic acid and lowers pH.</p>

      <div class="pyq-tag">Question 6</div>
      <p>The Paris Agreement was adopted in:</p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>A. 1992</li><li>B. 1997</li><li>C. 2005</li><li>D. 2015</li></ul>
      <div class="answer-box">Answer: D. 2015</div>
      <p class="explain">Explanation: The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015 (entered into force in 2016).</p></div>

      <div id="one-page-revision" class="topic-card"><div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">15</span><h2>One-Page Revision — एक पेज में पुनरावृत्ति</h2></div>
      <div class="formula-box">Greenhouse Effect = Natural Warming | Global Warming = Enhanced GHG Effect</div>
      <div class="formula-box">Climate Change = Global Warming + Weather Pattern Shifts</div>

      <p><strong>GHGs (GWP):</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>CO₂ = 1, CH₄ = 25, N₂O = 298, CFCs = thousands</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Mitigation vs Adaptation:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Mitigation = Reduce emissions (cause)</li>
      <li>Adaptation = Manage impacts (effect)</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Ozone Depletion:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Cause: CFCs | Treaty: Montreal Protocol (1987)</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Climate Agreements:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>UNFCCC (1992): Framework</li>
      <li>Kyoto Protocol (1997): Binding targets for developed</li>
      <li>Paris Agreement (2015): All countries, NDCs, 1.5°C-2°C</li></ul>

      <p><strong>CBDR-RC:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Biodiversity Loss:</strong></p>
      <ul class="bullet-list"><li>Causes: Habitat loss, climate change, pollution, overexploitation, invasive species</li></ul>

      <p><strong>Exam Formula:</strong></p>
      <div class="formula-box">GHG ↑ = Warming ↑ = Climate Change ↑ = Impacts ↑</div>
      <p><em>UGC NET में Climate Change & Global Environmental Issues के questions GHGs, agreements, ozone depletion, ocean acidification, और CBDR-RC पर based होते हैं।</em></p></div>

